1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,780
equilibrium before we proceed.

2
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:07,780
So this is a macro model, but it's a general equilibrium

3
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:08,480
macro model.

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,020
And in fact, when you think about it,

5
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:12,520
every general equilibrium model is a kind of macro model

6
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,940
because it's describing what happens to an entire economy.

7
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:15,440
Right?

8
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:16,900
Sounds good.

9
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:17,400
All right.

10
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:18,980
So anyway, common in macroeconomics

11
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,000
to analyze consumption over time.

12
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,420
So how can you move from analysis

13
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:24,140
of an individual or a small country

14
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:26,700
to analysis of general equilibrium

15
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,500
to a whole country or the world?

16
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,740
So do you guys do small country models in macro at some point?

17
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:35,700
And basically, this is where you apply

18
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,320
for a perfect competition of countries.

19
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:38,940
You say you're a small country, that's

20
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:41,580
like being one firm in front of the competitive market.

21
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:42,080
All right.

22
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:43,300
So anyway.

23
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,820
So the trick is we want to find whatever prices are required

24
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,060
to induce agri-consumption to equal agri-endowments.

25
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:52,820
People cannot consume more than exists,

26
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:56,140
and why shouldn't they consume all that exists?

27
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:57,380
Why not?

28
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:58,020
All right.

29
00:00:58,020 --> 00:00:59,780
And then in intertemporal markets,

30
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:01,940
we make special name for the price.

31
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:04,340
So if you want to buy some money today,

32
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:06,700
what kind of a price do you pay?

33
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,740
Yes, you pay interest.

34
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:10,540
Just a price.

35
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:12,260
Right?

36
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:13,260
So interest rates.

37
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:14,220
OK.

38
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:14,720
All right.

39
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,220
So suppose we've got a world that's

40
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:19,580
populated by identical infinitely-lived agents.

41
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:22,300
So one of the very realistic assumptions

42
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,340
that we usually make, but it gives you some ideas.

43
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:27,300
So every one of these individuals

44
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,180
lives forever, and they got a utility function

45
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:36,740
given by the infinite sum of beta raised to the power of t

46
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:39,860
times the log of consumption in period t.

47
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:41,900
So you can write this out in long-hand.

48
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:43,460
This is saying that utility is going

49
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:47,580
to be equal to the log of consumption in period 0

50
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:50,780
plus beta times the log of consumption in period 1

51
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:54,100
plus beta squared times the log of consumption in period 2.

52
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:55,500
And so.

53
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,740
That doesn't make any sense, that's just the long-hand

54
00:01:57,740 --> 00:01:59,580
version of that.

55
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:02,700
All right, and then I will just tell you the standard result.

56
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,420
So the standard result is that every individual

57
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:08,220
wants to set consumption in time t plus 1

58
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:14,420
equal to beta times 1 plus r, their consumption in period t.

59
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:16,340
So whatever is going on, you want

60
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:17,900
to go and arrange your consumption

61
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:19,940
to fit this pattern.

62
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:24,460
So intuitively, this means that if you have a very low beta,

63
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:26,740
then you know, so very low beta means that you

64
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:29,780
are patient or impatient.

65
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:30,740
We'll take a look here.

66
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:33,340
What does a 0 beta mean?

67
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:34,300
What does 0 beta mean?

68
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:36,780
What counts?

69
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:37,740
Only the first period.

70
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:40,180
Only today and the future, hell with the future.

71
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,100
All right, on the other hand, if beta were 1,

72
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:44,540
then what does that mean?

73
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:46,740
Future counts just as much as the present.

74
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:49,060
So high beta means you're patient or impatient?

75
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:49,900
Patient.

76
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,060
Yeah, high beta means that you are patient.

77
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:52,700
OK.

78
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,860
So if you've got a high beta, then

79
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:56,740
that means that you are going to tend

80
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:59,900
to be consuming more in the future.

81
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,380
On the other hand, if you've got a low beta,

82
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:04,780
then you're going to be consuming less in the future.

83
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:06,900
And then, of course, this 1 plus r,

84
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,700
so where r is the interest rate.

85
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:11,180
So that means that when interest rates are high,

86
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:16,180
then you are tending to defer your consumption to the future.

87
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:18,700
So it means that because there's a high interest rate,

88
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:21,540
you may as well restrict what you're consuming now

89
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:24,540
and then wait, leave it in the bank or wherever,

90
00:03:24,540 --> 00:03:25,260
and let it grow.

91
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:27,900
And then you could have a bright future for yourself.

92
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:28,780
OK.

93
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:30,620
And then, of course, if this thing here

94
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:33,580
happens to be less than 1, then you

95
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:36,820
have a permanently declining consumption profile.

96
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:39,140
So you're consuming less and less every period.

97
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:41,740
And on the other hand, if this thing is greater than 1,

98
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,300
that means you're consuming more and more every period.

99
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:45,180
OK.

100
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:46,540
All right, but so far, we've just

101
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:49,820
done an easy intermediate micro or intermediate micro

102
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:52,300
or micro macro problem.

103
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:54,100
So this is one where we're just figuring out

104
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:55,460
what one individual does.

105
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:56,900
But this is a PhD class.

106
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:59,180
And so we figure out what is implied

107
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:03,860
if we figure out what happens to the entire society given

108
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:06,300
these assumptions.

109
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:07,820
And, of course, general equilibrium

110
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:10,780
says that every period, the amount that you consume,

111
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:15,380
this is an economy without any storage, by the way.

112
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:16,780
So it says that every period, you

113
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:18,900
got to consume whatever it is that you produce.

114
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:22,380
So if, for example, people have constant endowments,

115
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:28,740
so if you got a constant endowment,

116
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:31,740
it's like every period you get 10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10,

117
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:32,940
forever.

118
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:35,100
If everyone's got the same preferences,

119
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:40,500
then we need to figure out for what value of beta times 1

120
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:43,380
plus r do we get an amount equal to 1?

121
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:45,580
There's the same amount available in every period.

122
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:47,960
Then people better actually be consuming the same amount

123
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:50,780
every single period, right?

124
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:54,100
So because otherwise, we will either

125
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:57,180
be consuming more than we have, which is impossible,

126
00:04:57,180 --> 00:04:59,680
or we'll be consuming less than we have, which is pointless.

127
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:01,020
This stuff is just going to rot.

128
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:03,340
It's just going to waste.

129
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:06,220
So if we got constant endowments and we know that we got beta,

130
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,540
we get 1 plus r.

131
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,260
So that has to equal 1.

132
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:13,260
And then we are one step away from an amazing PhD result

133
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:16,300
reached solely through algebra.

134
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:18,860
So remember, which of these variables

135
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:23,100
is supposed to be fixed and which one fluctuates

136
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:24,940
in response to the fixed thing?

137
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:27,940
So which thing is supposed to be a fact in the world

138
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:31,180
and which one is a flexible thing about society?

139
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:31,980
Beta is fixed.

140
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:32,900
Beta is fixed.

141
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:35,780
So people are either patient or impatient.

142
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:38,100
That's their preference.

143
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:43,060
So the thing that can vary, though, as usual, is the price.

144
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:44,900
So then all you have to do is go and solve,

145
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:47,420
and you'll see that we get 1 plus r is going

146
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:49,660
to be equal to 1 over beta.

147
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:51,300
And that's a general equilibrium result.

148
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:54,740
This tells us if everyone follows these assumptions,

149
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:58,780
what is the result that allows society to function

150
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:01,100
in a consistent way, in a way consistent with everyone

151
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:03,780
and everyone maximizing their own utility?

152
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:04,580
It's a simple one.

153
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:07,820
So 1 plus r equals 1 over beta, which, again,

154
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:09,660
is super intuitive when you think about it.

155
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:11,420
Because remember, so a high beta means

156
00:06:11,420 --> 00:06:14,220
you're patient or impatient.

157
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:16,300
High beta means you are patient.

158
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,380
So if you've got high beta, then what is the interest rate

159
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:21,500
going to look like?

160
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:25,060
High beta means high or low interest?

161
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:25,780
Low interest.

162
00:06:25,780 --> 00:06:27,740
High patience, low interest.

163
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:33,020
All right, and on the other hand, suppose beta were 0.01,

164
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:35,420
then we need to have an astronomical interest rate

165
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:37,940
to convince these super impatient people to save.

166
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:42,660
So that's the idea.

167
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:45,660
And then imagine a world where you've got growing endowments.

168
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:48,380
Like suppose you've got technological progress, which

169
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:50,180
is just a built-in fact of the universe.

170
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:51,840
Every year, the technological progress,

171
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,820
people are getting richer and richer over time.

172
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:56,260
So if you've got endowments growing at a rate of, say,

173
00:06:56,260 --> 00:07:00,300
1 plus g, where g is the rate of growth of endowments,

174
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:02,780
then people are going to want a smooth consumption

175
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:04,860
by borrowing against the future income.

176
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:07,980
Suppose you know that you've got an inheritance coming, say.

177
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:09,940
What do you want to do today if you know you're

178
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:12,420
going to inherit next year?

179
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:12,920
Borrow.

180
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:14,900
Borrow against that inheritance.

181
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:16,980
And if there is this fundamental economic growth

182
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:19,700
built into your future, then why?

183
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:21,500
You say, well, I don't have to wait in order

184
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:23,780
to enjoy the future today.

185
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:26,940
I'll just borrow against the future, which

186
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:29,220
works perfectly fine for any one human being.

187
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:32,100
But can the entire society borrow against the future?

188
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:35,020
Is there a time machine for goods where we can go forward?

189
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:35,700
No.

190
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:38,940
So if everyone is trying to borrow against the future,

191
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:40,620
then what happens is not the people

192
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:43,540
get to consume more than exists today, which is impossible.

193
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:48,020
What it means is that something, the great social mechanism

194
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:51,020
for changing people's minds, but it has to fill the gap.

195
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:52,660
And what's that great social mechanism?

196
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:53,160
Price.

197
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:53,660
Price.

198
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:54,140
Right.

199
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:55,820
So people know they're going to be rich in the future.

200
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:56,500
What do you need to do?

201
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:57,420
What do you have to do?

202
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:59,500
What does the industry have to do to persuade them

203
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:00,540
to not consume it?

204
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:01,040
Price.

205
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:01,900
It's got to rise.

206
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,820
So people know, well, we're going to get rich.

207
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:04,460
Time to borrow?

208
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:06,160
Oh, my god, the interest rate is so high,

209
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:08,460
I don't want to do it anymore.

210
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:11,260
So if we have a built-in rate of growth of endowments of 1 plus

211
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:14,940
g, then we'll see that the interest rate is going

212
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:17,180
to be equal to 1 plus r, or 1 plus r

213
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:20,260
is going to be equal to 1 plus g over beta.

214
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:23,700
So expected growth raises interest rates.

215
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:26,260
Expected growth raises interest rates.

216
00:08:26,260 --> 00:08:30,380
It's a basic result here.

217
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:32,980
Now, if you know anything about the history of interest rates,

218
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:35,980
this raises interesting issues, because what

219
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:38,660
is the general pattern over the last couple thousand years

220
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:39,220
of interest?

221
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:42,340
Have interest rates gone up, down, or stayed the same?

222
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:45,140
They've gone way down, tremendously down.

223
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:48,700
And what was economic growth thousands of years ago again?

224
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:50,020
About zero.

225
00:08:50,020 --> 00:08:53,260
So, hmm, well, does it look like it's the g, the method,

226
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:54,420
that's doing the work then?

227
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:56,140
The g actually cuts the other way.

228
00:08:56,140 --> 00:08:58,220
The g should have raised our interest rates.

229
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:01,420
So what is at least the simple model

230
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:04,020
says what's happened to patients over time?

231
00:09:04,020 --> 00:09:08,140
Yeah, we've gotten tremendously more patient than we used to be.

232
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:09,140
Is that the right story?

233
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:12,540
I think that's at least a start.

234
00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:15,660
And so maybe, maybe that's the right story.

235
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,500
Now, again, we start complicating the model.

236
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:18,940
You could also add in other things,

237
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:21,420
like maybe there's a greater fall risk in the past,

238
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:25,060
like the world is so dangerous that people die a lot

239
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:26,500
before they pay you back.

240
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:27,780
There's all kinds of disasters.

241
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:30,660
The mic's right, so maybe that's another big part of it.

242
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:31,160
Yes?

243
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:35,500
Do you think a lot of it could also be just life expectancy?

244
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,140
So life expectancy could do, let's see,

245
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:39,820
so what should life expectancy, let's see.

246
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:42,220
Like you're almost forced to be more patient because you've

247
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:43,980
got a longer span.

248
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:46,020
Yeah, so yeah, so if you knew that you were going to die

249
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:47,860
the day you stopped being productive,

250
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:49,860
then that's a reason to save less, right?

251
00:09:49,860 --> 00:09:51,580
Yeah, so that could work.

252
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:53,660
Although, again, a little bit complicated

253
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:57,140
because we've got, we definitely live longer,

254
00:09:57,140 --> 00:09:59,700
but we also have more years of working life, right?

255
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:01,300
And those extra years of working life

256
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:05,260
means that you expect to have a larger lifetime wealth.

257
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:06,940
So it's complicated.

258
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:08,020
All right.

259
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:10,380
Anyway, so this is just another application

260
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:13,040
of general equilibrium showing you can easily take it over

261
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:14,020
to a macro model.

262
00:10:14,020 --> 00:10:16,900
And it's in the late 70s, early 80s,

263
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:19,540
the macro models like this became really popular.

264
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:22,060
You may have even done some.

265
00:10:22,060 --> 00:10:23,100
All right, questions?

266
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:25,540
Are you happy?

267
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:26,340
OK.

268
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:29,380
All right, now we're going to start with game theory.

269
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:31,060
All right, so how many people here

270
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:33,140
have done game theory at some point in your life?

271
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:33,820
All right, good.

272
00:10:33,820 --> 00:10:35,900
All right, so a lot of this will be review.

273
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:38,020
Hopefully, we will take it to a higher level.

274
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:39,700
And yet, I will not torture you with math

275
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:41,240
that you'll never need to know again.

276
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:43,620
I'm going to try to give you enough math

277
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:47,060
so that you understand the basics and can read papers,

278
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:49,540
and then also give you enough additional information

279
00:10:49,540 --> 00:10:52,260
that you could write papers and do research.

280
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:54,100
So we'll see.

281
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:55,900
All right.

282
00:10:55,900 --> 00:11:00,300
So as I said, with general equilibrium theory,

283
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:02,020
you can go pretty far.

284
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:05,180
So we can analyze an economy with any number of people,

285
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:07,820
any number of goods, any number of time periods,

286
00:11:07,820 --> 00:11:10,100
any number of states of affairs.

287
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:13,100
So that sounds like we've got a pretty big set

288
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:14,940
of possibilities.

289
00:11:14,940 --> 00:11:17,900
But in the end, most people who go explore general equilibrium

290
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:20,820
long enough decide that there's too many questions that it

291
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:22,020
leaves unanswered.

292
00:11:22,020 --> 00:11:24,220
There is a well-known book by Fisher Black,

293
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:26,340
you may have heard of him, called Exploring General

294
00:11:26,340 --> 00:11:27,020
Equilibrium.

295
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:30,420
And this is what's called an idiosyncratic book.

296
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:33,500
This is a book where a famous economist just says, look,

297
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,700
I'm going to explain everything in the whole universe

298
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:37,940
using general equilibrium theory.

299
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:40,180
Try to stop me.

300
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:42,420
And it's the kind of book you're like, well,

301
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:43,660
it's a very interesting book.

302
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:47,020
And it's a powerful intellect behind it,

303
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:50,100
somewhat dogmatic or maybe incredibly dogmatic.

304
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:52,900
But still, there are a lot of people who read it

305
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:55,860
and just kind of liked it.

306
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:56,540
It's different.

307
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:57,660
Anyway.

308
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:00,460
But well, let's take a regret in the Fisher Black's world

309
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:01,940
and move on.

310
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:05,540
So what most people find general equilibrium very unsatisfying.

311
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:07,620
And because in the real world, people usually

312
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:10,740
stand somewhere in between a simple one-agent world,

313
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,780
where it's like me hunting crawfish, where I figure out

314
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:15,540
what the crawfish do, and then I eat them,

315
00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:17,700
and the crawfish keep being idiots.

316
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:19,020
So that's one world.

317
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:21,020
And the other world is the infinite agent world,

318
00:12:21,020 --> 00:12:24,140
or the near infinite agent world that we've been talking about.

319
00:12:24,140 --> 00:12:26,540
And that's another one.

320
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:28,820
And then furthermore, even when you start out

321
00:12:28,820 --> 00:12:32,980
in this near infinite agent case, a lot of times in the end,

322
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:34,620
things get narrowed down.

323
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:36,700
So think about what life is like, first of all,

324
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:38,420
for a single person.

325
00:12:38,420 --> 00:12:41,060
So even easier in the world of online dating.

326
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:45,820
So how many people are available for online dating to you?

327
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:48,060
Tens of millions of people.

328
00:12:48,060 --> 00:12:51,140
So it seems like general equilibrium is about right.

329
00:12:51,140 --> 00:12:53,820
And I don't think anyone is saying, well, look,

330
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:56,300
if I'm a bad date, this might lead

331
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:59,700
to a general decline in the quality of my opportunities.

332
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:02,020
So I'd better be very careful here,

333
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:05,500
lest I unseat the social balance.

334
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:08,020
So probably no one, when they're doing online dating,

335
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:10,100
is worrying that they personally are

336
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:12,780
going to change the way that the world of dating works.

337
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:15,140
No one says, look, if I'm really nice,

338
00:13:15,140 --> 00:13:18,060
this could lead to a general rise in expectations.

339
00:13:18,060 --> 00:13:19,660
So even though in the short run, it

340
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:22,740
may appear that I benefit from my reputation

341
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:24,820
for being a good person, in the long run,

342
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:27,300
I will have so interfered with the market

343
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:29,740
that my opportunities will be depressed and limited.

344
00:13:29,740 --> 00:13:33,100
All right, so this would be what a megalomaniac would think.

345
00:13:33,100 --> 00:13:35,060
But anyway, that same person says,

346
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:36,700
I'm just one little person here.

347
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:38,660
The world does not revolve around me.

348
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:40,700
So I can just assume that whatever I do

349
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:44,100
has no effect on general behavior.

350
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:45,980
So that's what it's like to be single.

351
00:13:45,980 --> 00:13:47,580
On the other hand, suppose that you've

352
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:49,580
been married for 10 years, you have three kids,

353
00:13:49,580 --> 00:13:52,900
and then you're thinking, well, look,

354
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:55,980
I don't need to worry about being mistreated by my spouse

355
00:13:55,980 --> 00:13:58,740
because the market.

356
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:00,940
There are millions of other people I could be dating.

357
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:03,900
So if my spouse were to give me anything 1%

358
00:14:03,900 --> 00:14:07,060
below my opportunity cost, then this

359
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:08,560
would lead to immediate dissolution.

360
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:11,460
I'd switch to another person, and problem solved.

361
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:14,620
So once you have put a lot of investment in the relationship,

362
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:17,140
the fact that there's lots of other opportunities

363
00:14:17,140 --> 00:14:18,780
is no longer very much protection.

364
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:21,340
On the other hand, if you were to say, look,

365
00:14:21,340 --> 00:14:23,980
I mean, there'd be no point in me being 1% nicer,

366
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:25,980
because all that would happen was I would then

367
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:27,780
get a million other dating opportunities.

368
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:31,980
So there's no real reason for me to give flowers like an extra

369
00:14:31,980 --> 00:14:34,380
just because of flowers to your spouse.

370
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:36,880
And of course, this is not what people who are married generally

371
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:38,060
think is going on.

372
00:14:38,060 --> 00:14:41,220
Instead, they think, we both have a lot invested here.

373
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:45,380
So on the one hand, either of us could fall quite short,

374
00:14:45,380 --> 00:14:47,340
and still we'd probably stay together.

375
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:50,420
Or on the other hand, one of us could go and improve a lot

376
00:14:50,420 --> 00:14:54,900
and would not automatically lead to any better treatments.

377
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:58,100
And then, well, simple, if I started to say,

378
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:00,260
I cut back my wasteful spending, would this

379
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:03,220
lead my spouse to cut back their wasteful spending?

380
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:07,420
Or maybe they would just gobble up more of the free resources?

381
00:15:07,420 --> 00:15:08,740
What's going to happen here?

382
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:09,860
And this is true even though there

383
00:15:09,860 --> 00:15:11,320
was one point in your life when you

384
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:12,780
had millions of opportunities.

385
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:15,020
But once you have been together for quite a while

386
00:15:15,020 --> 00:15:16,900
and made some common investments,

387
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:23,020
then the market no longer is all that relevant.

388
00:15:23,020 --> 00:15:25,460
You say, well, things got extreme enough.

389
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:26,300
Then it would matter.

390
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:29,180
Yeah, so it's not saying the market doesn't matter at all.

391
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:32,820
So your opportunity costs or your outside opportunities

392
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:34,380
still matter to some degree.

393
00:15:34,380 --> 00:15:37,460
But within a big range, they might be totally irrelevant.

394
00:15:37,460 --> 00:15:41,180
And then you sit there saying, well, what am I going to do now?

395
00:15:41,180 --> 00:15:44,780
Do I just sit here and listen to my mother-in-law insult me?

396
00:15:44,780 --> 00:15:46,340
I mean, if this were a first date

397
00:15:46,340 --> 00:15:49,620
and I were being insulted by my date's mother,

398
00:15:49,620 --> 00:15:52,380
I'd probably just decide to move on.

399
00:15:52,380 --> 00:15:53,820
But now I'm at a point in my life

400
00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:55,740
where maybe I just need to sit here and shut up

401
00:15:55,740 --> 00:15:57,980
and take the abuse.

402
00:15:57,980 --> 00:16:01,820
OK, nothing to do with me.

403
00:16:01,820 --> 00:16:06,580
TV, movies, this is all my life experience.

404
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:11,180
Once in a while, someone opens up to me, but not often.

405
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:14,460
One friend who told me stuff.

406
00:16:14,460 --> 00:16:17,460
That's what's going on now.

407
00:16:17,460 --> 00:16:19,260
So game theory tries to analyze situations

408
00:16:19,260 --> 00:16:22,900
where strategy does matter and usually ends up

409
00:16:22,900 --> 00:16:25,460
with answers that are a lot vaguer or foggier

410
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:27,100
than general equilibrium.

411
00:16:27,100 --> 00:16:29,300
But at least a lot of people think game theory is more

412
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:30,980
realistic.

413
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:34,540
And there is a line from famous behavioral economist

414
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:36,900
and now Nobel Prize winner Richard Thaler, which

415
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:40,620
is I'd rather be vaguely right than clearly wrong.

416
00:16:40,620 --> 00:16:43,140
And this is a motivation behind a lot of game theory

417
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:46,580
compared to general equilibrium.

418
00:16:46,580 --> 00:16:47,460
So questions?

419
00:16:47,460 --> 00:16:48,540
Everyone happy?

420
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:49,040
Satisfied?

421
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:49,740
All right, good.

422
00:16:53,540 --> 00:16:54,700
So starting with game theory.

423
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:58,900
So standard consumer theory provides the building blocks.

424
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:01,740
So game theory retains the assumption

425
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:04,060
that people have utility functions.

426
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:06,260
Everybody's got a utility function.

427
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:08,140
Sometimes game theorists talk about payoffs

428
00:17:08,140 --> 00:17:09,340
instead of utility.

429
00:17:09,340 --> 00:17:10,780
It's the same thing.

430
00:17:10,780 --> 00:17:12,100
Exactly the same thing.

431
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:13,680
So you have the constant and the same.

432
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:15,980
If you have a choice of different payoffs, which one

433
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:16,500
do you take?

434
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:17,020
The largest.

435
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,660
Just like if you have a choice of different things

436
00:17:18,660 --> 00:17:21,060
that give you different utilities, which do you do?

437
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:22,900
I think it gives you the highest utility.

438
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:24,540
All right, now I'm going to say something

439
00:17:24,540 --> 00:17:26,820
that you don't need to know until after the midterm.

440
00:17:26,820 --> 00:17:30,300
But if you start getting theoretically nervous,

441
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:33,460
like there's something that I'm doing that is not OK.

442
00:17:33,460 --> 00:17:36,140
It is OK, actually.

443
00:17:36,140 --> 00:17:37,700
So why is it OK?

444
00:17:37,700 --> 00:17:40,940
It's OK because actually it is normal to interpret payoffs

445
00:17:40,940 --> 00:17:44,780
as von Neumann-Morgenstern expected utility functions.

446
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:47,900
If you don't know what von Neumann-Morgenstern expected

447
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:49,780
utility functions are, well, these

448
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:54,860
are utility functions named after the Nazi, Dr. Strangelove.

449
00:17:54,860 --> 00:17:56,140
Yeah, he was a Nazi.

450
00:17:56,140 --> 00:17:59,140
He was a rocket scientist who worked for Nazis.

451
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:04,180
Anyway, so that guy was based on John von Neumann.

452
00:18:04,180 --> 00:18:06,860
In any case, we're going to talk about the details of what

453
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:09,660
these utility functions are like after the midterm.

454
00:18:09,660 --> 00:18:12,660
But if there's ever a point where you think, wait a second,

455
00:18:12,660 --> 00:18:14,980
are you assuming risk neutrality or anything like that?

456
00:18:14,980 --> 00:18:16,620
No, no, no, actually I'm just assuming

457
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:18,540
that they are von Neumann-Morgenstern expected

458
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:19,740
utility functions.

459
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:23,220
And if you are really bothered by what

460
00:18:23,220 --> 00:18:26,060
seems like the shaky theoretical foundations,

461
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:29,340
you can read ahead about the old risk media article

462
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:32,140
on von Neumann-Morgenstern expected utility functions.

463
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:33,940
If you are content that what I'm doing

464
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:36,980
is going to be justified later on,

465
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:40,740
then we'll just move through and you don't need to worry about it.

466
00:18:40,740 --> 00:18:43,860
Either way, we're OK.

467
00:18:43,860 --> 00:18:45,020
So again, why?

468
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:48,060
Because von Neumann-Morgenstern expected utility sideship

469
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:51,500
all issues of risk aversion by building it into the numbers.

470
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:53,500
And if you're confused by how this is possible,

471
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:54,380
don't worry about it.

472
00:18:54,380 --> 00:18:57,380
I'm going to talk about it later.

473
00:18:57,380 --> 00:19:00,020
So now, there's two ways the identity game

474
00:19:00,020 --> 00:19:01,260
can be represented.

475
00:19:01,260 --> 00:19:03,300
And these are known as the extensive form

476
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:05,300
and the normal form.

477
00:19:05,300 --> 00:19:08,380
Extensive form and normal.

478
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:10,420
So what does an extensive form show?

479
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:13,540
It shows every possible thing that can happen in the game

480
00:19:13,540 --> 00:19:15,060
turn by turn.

481
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:18,060
Every possible thing that can happen in the game turn by turn.

482
00:19:18,060 --> 00:19:21,420
So it shows how behavior branches out from choice nodes.

483
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:22,980
So a dot, that's a node.

484
00:19:22,980 --> 00:19:25,700
This is a chance for someone to make a decision.

485
00:19:25,700 --> 00:19:28,020
And then maybe that person has three decisions.

486
00:19:28,020 --> 00:19:29,540
And then how do we draw that?

487
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:31,300
By showing three lines.

488
00:19:31,300 --> 00:19:33,260
And then where do the lines lead?

489
00:19:33,260 --> 00:19:34,940
To another node.

490
00:19:34,940 --> 00:19:36,340
Lines lead to another node.

491
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:39,500
And at that next node, someone else, usually another player,

492
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:40,540
will get to make choices.

493
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:43,260
Maybe there could be two choices there, and only one choice there,

494
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:46,100
and five choices there.

495
00:19:46,100 --> 00:19:50,500
And then each of those leads to a node, which then leads

496
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:55,020
to other possible actions.

497
00:19:55,020 --> 00:19:57,940
And then finally, at the end, we go and show the payoffs

498
00:19:57,940 --> 00:20:21,500
for all the players.


